// The bootstrap interpreter runs every worker on one host thread, so a call
// of class blocks blocks every worker (spec/README.md, Conformance, Parallel
// workers). This probe checks the bootstrap's own choice: a `run` on a worker
// also blocks the main task. The call is of class blocks, and not a sleep: a
// sleep is of class suspends, and it lets the other tasks run.
//
// The worker wakes the main task with a send, then waits for a process that
// sleeps. Under the one-thread form the main task cannot run before the
// process ends, so the first receive returns only after it. An implementation
// that blocks only the worker of the call returns from the receive at once.
fun sleeper(main) {
    task.send(main, "awake");
    run("sh", ["-c", "sleep 1"]);
    task.send(main, "slept");
}

var start = time();
task.spawn_worker(sleeper, task.self());
print task.receive();
print time() - start >= 0.5;
print task.receive();

// STATUS: ok
// CHECK: awake
// CHECK: true
// CHECK: slept
